  <!-- Dialogs -->
  <Dialog id="kx-method" title="How the study was built" eyebrow="analyze.py · data/">
    <p><b>Data.</b> The June 30 2026 slate: six games, 1-minute candlesticks for each game's moneyline (KXMLBGAME) and totals (KXMLBTOTAL) market from Kalshi, and play-by-play from the MLB Stats API. 455 plate-appearance events, each aligned to the candle grid by the play's end time. Per-event results are in <code>data/event_study.csv</code>, the per-type summary in <code>data/event_summary.csv</code>.</p>
    <p><b>Windows.</b> Minute 0 is the first candle boundary at or after the play ends. The baseline is the candle that closed one minute before it; immediate is the change from the baseline to two minutes after; drift is the change from two to seven minutes after. Signs are from the batting team's perspective, so a positive number is the market moving toward the team at bat. The reaction-path chart uses the candle two minutes before as its baseline, as the repository figure does.</p>
    <p><b>Volume and spread.</b> The volume spike is the three-minute volume after the play relative to the game's baseline (three times the median per-minute volume), summarised as the median ratio per event type. Spread is the mean ask minus bid over the same three minutes.</p>
    <p><b>Tests.</b> p-values are one-sample t-tests of the immediate move against zero. The momentum test is the Pearson correlation between the immediate move and the drift on events with an immediate move of at least 3¢ in either direction.</p>
    <p><b>Event groups.</b> Home run; double and triple; single; walk, hit by pitch and intentional walk; strikeout, including strikeout double plays; outs in play (field outs, force outs, double plays, sacrifices, fielder's choice). "Any scoring play" is every event that scored a run, whatever its type.</p>
  </Dialog>

  <Dialog id="kx-risk" title="Risk layer" eyebrow="crates/common/src/risk.rs">
    <p>A pure module: no networking and no order transmission. A live executor must call <code>RiskBook::approve</code> before every order and honour every veto. The defaults are the Phase 6 starting values from the cutover plan and change only by editing config and rebuilding. In the author's words, there is no bypass: friction here is the feature.</p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table class="table">
        <thead><tr><th>Limit</th><th class="num">Default</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><code>max_exposure_cents</code></td><td class="num">2,500¢</td><td>$25 total cost basis across all open positions</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>per_market_cap_cents</code></td><td class="num">500¢</td><td>$5 in any single market</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>daily_loss_stop_cents</code></td><td class="num">500¢</td><td>a realised loss of $5 locks trading out for the day</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>max_orders_per_min</code></td><td class="num">10</td><td>order-placement cap over a trailing 60 seconds</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>max_price_distance_cents</code></td><td class="num">5¢</td><td>reject orders priced further than this from the current mid</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <h3>Vetoes</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>PriceBounds, BadCount:</b> a price outside 1 to 99¢ or a non-positive contract count.</li>
      <li><b>DailyLoss:</b> realised P&amp;L for the day at or past the stop.</li>
      <li><b>NoMid:</b> no fresh, finite mid for the market. A missing or NaN price vetoes the order.</li>
      <li><b>PriceDistance, MarketCap, ExposureCap:</b> the caps count filled and in-flight (approved but unfilled) exposure, so a burst of approvals cannot pass one by one and blow through the limit once they all fill.</li>
      <li><b>OrderRate:</b> more than the cap in the trailing window.</li>
    </ul>
    <h3>Durable stop decisions</h3>
    <p>When the daily stop fires, the executor writes a <code>LOCKOUT-&lt;date&gt;</code> file, flattens and exits. On start-up a locked-out day refuses to run, so a launchd KeepAlive supervisor can never resurrect the bot into a day it already lost. A kill file and a dead-man switch on stale book or game feeds complete the set. A veto is logged and skipped; the executor never retries around one.</p>
  </Dialog>

  <Dialog id="kx-exec" title="Live execution spec" eyebrow="docs/LIVE_EXECUTION_SPEC.md" wide>
    <p>The contract for an order-execution layer that is deliberately not built. Request signing, read-only account views, fill parsing, the risk layer and the strategy signals exist and are tested in <code>common</code> and <code>paper-trader</code>; the executor itself is left to the human operator.</p>
    <h3>The two calls</h3>
<pre><code>POST /trade-api/v2/portfolio/orders
&#123; "ticker": "...", "client_order_id": "&lt;uuid you generate&gt;",
  "side": "yes", "action": "buy", "count": 1, "type": "limit", "yes_price": 42 &#125;

DELETE /trade-api/v2/portfolio/orders/&#123;order_id&#125;</code></pre>
    <h3>Invariants</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Idempotency.</b> One <code>client_order_id</code> per intent, reused verbatim on every retry. A timeout is not a failure: the order is possibly live until <code>resting_orders()</code> or the fills channel says otherwise.</li>
      <li><b>Two-key turn.</b> Absent host keys default to production, so the executor must refuse to start when <code>live_enabled = true</code> and the API base is the production host unless <code>confirm_prod = true</code> is also set.</li>
      <li><b>Fills only.</b> Position and cost basis change only on fill events from the websocket, never on send. The strategy never calls the API; it emits desired state and the executor owns all transmission.</li>
      <li><b>Start-up.</b> Refuse to run on a locked-out day; cancel every resting order and refetch until none remain; adopt or flatten existing positions; refuse to start below a balance floor.</li>
    </ul>
    <h3>Gates before real money</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>G2:</b> place, query, cancel and fill observed on the demo host; a retried client_order_id provably does not double-fill.</li>
      <li><b>G3:</b> kill -9 mid-position five times, clean reconciliation every time.</li>
      <li><b>G5:</b> at least 200 demo trades, positive P&amp;L, shadow-versus-live slippage quantified with compare_fills.py.</li>
      <li><b>G6:</b> one-contract sizing for a supervised week with a penny-perfect ledger against the Kalshi UI; scale only by a rule written down in advance.</li>
    </ul>
  </Dialog>

  <script src="../../scripts/kalshi-page.ts"></script>
  <script src="../../scripts/kalshi-runners.ts"></script>
</Base>

